  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=x-2y+\\frac{3}{2},\\,y(1)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $\u0394x=\\frac{1}{2},$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\n\u0394y=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{\u2032}+8{y}^{\u2032}-{y}_{-1}^{\u2032})\\cdot \u0394x\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{\u2032}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{\u2032}\\,$ | $\u0394{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{\u2032}$ | $\u0394y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{9}{16}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{17}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{8}$ | $\\frac{9}{32}$ | $\\frac{43}{32}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | {{field-1}} |",
